1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:06,537
One last sweep of the brush, and what it uncovers is the brush itself, and the trowel, set down.

2
00:00:09,167 --> 00:00:15,949
Forty-three episodes ago we started with the question of how anybody knows anything about a period with no newspapers,

3
00:00:15,949 --> 00:00:18,286
no archives and no surviving eyewitness.

4
00:00:18,286 --> 00:00:22,177
This episode is the answer, walked through one case at a time.

5
00:00:25,100 --> 00:00:28,196
All five, for the only time in the series.

6
00:00:28,196 --> 00:00:29,276
Things dug up.

7
00:00:29,276 --> 00:00:29,780
Texts.

8
00:00:29,780 --> 00:00:30,787
Inscriptions.

9
00:00:30,787 --> 00:00:31,291
Coins.

10
00:00:31,291 --> 00:00:32,875
Travellers' accounts.

11
00:00:32,875 --> 00:00:35,683
No single one of them would be enough.

12
00:00:35,683 --> 00:00:43,326
Every episode in this series has been an exercise in putting at least two of them against each other.

13
00:00:44,167 --> 00:00:45,155
The first room.

14
00:00:45,155 --> 00:00:48,491
A hand axe, and the long prehistory that produced it.

15
00:00:48,491 --> 00:00:50,839
A painted pot, and the first farmers.

16
00:00:50,839 --> 00:00:54,546
Then the Harappan cases: the brick and the drain, the seal,

17
00:00:54,546 --> 00:01:00,715
the bronze figurine — a civilisation of cities we can see in detail and cannot read a word of.

18
00:01:04,133 --> 00:01:12,315
Then the Vedic rooms: a palm-leaf manuscript, painted grey ware, a fire altar, and a burial stone from the megalithic south.

19
00:01:13,800 --> 00:01:15,042
The second stretch.

20
00:01:15,042 --> 00:01:21,063
A punch-marked coin and a city wall, as cities and money return together after a thousand years.

21
00:01:21,063 --> 00:01:29,186
A tree above an empty throne; a stupa; a votive tablet — the age of the Buddha and Mahavira, shown as that age showed itself.

22
00:01:31,633 --> 00:01:35,181
A Greek-style coin, for the arrivals from the north-west.

23
00:01:35,181 --> 00:01:41,291
And then the Mauryas: a manual of statecraft, a bull capital, a rock edict, a polished pillar.

24
00:01:44,167 --> 00:01:45,341
The third stretch.

25
00:01:45,341 --> 00:01:49,234
A carved gateway, and the merchants and monks who paid for it.

26
00:01:49,234 --> 00:01:54,239
Coins in two scripts, then Kushana gold, as the north-west becomes a crossroads.

27
00:01:54,239 --> 00:01:57,752
A hero stone and a Tamil manuscript, for the south.

28
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:04,424
A Roman amphora, carried thousands of miles and thrown away here.

29
00:02:04,424 --> 00:02:12,224
Then Gupta gold, a copper plate, an iron pillar that will not rust — and Kailasa, a temple made by taking a hillside away.

30
00:02:13,700 --> 00:02:15,223
And the last stretch.

31
00:02:15,223 --> 00:02:19,863
A Chola temple tower and a state that wrote its accounts on walls.

32
00:02:19,863 --> 00:02:21,248
A star-shaped plan.

33
00:02:21,248 --> 00:02:22,448
A hill fort.

34
00:02:23,967 --> 00:02:28,812
Then the thematic cases: a zero, a monastery seal, a traveller's staff,

35
00:02:28,812 --> 00:02:33,253
a boundary stone, a tray of coins, and a tablet of women's names.

36
00:02:33,253 --> 00:02:36,312
Forty-three cases, and the hall is full.

37
00:02:39,367 --> 00:02:42,862
One last disagreement, and it is about where we stopped.

38
00:02:42,862 --> 00:02:50,036
The conventional endpoint is around 1200 CE. But periodisation is a convenience imposed afterwards: regional states,

39
00:02:50,036 --> 00:02:58,185
land grants and temple building were well under way centuries before, and nothing about ordinary life changed in a single year.

40
00:03:00,133 --> 00:03:07,821
The three-part division of Indian history into ancient, medieval and modern was itself proposed in the colonial period on religious lines,

41
00:03:07,821 --> 00:03:11,028
and historians have largely moved away from that framing.

42
00:03:11,028 --> 00:03:16,085
Know the dates the syllabus uses, and know that they are a filing system, not a fact.

43
00:03:20,333 --> 00:03:24,078
Here is the last memory tip, carved in stone.

44
00:03:24,078 --> 00:03:26,764
The whole series in seven words.

45
00:03:26,764 --> 00:03:31,053
Stone, seed, city, state, faith, empire, region.

46
00:03:34,967 --> 00:03:38,408
A last Dig Quiz, and these three are about method.

47
00:03:38,408 --> 00:03:44,075
First: the script we can see clearly on thousands of objects and still cannot read.

48
00:03:44,075 --> 00:03:45,792
The Harappan script.

49
00:03:46,600 --> 00:03:52,093
Second: the kind of document that gives us a ruler's own words, dated and placed.

50
00:03:52,093 --> 00:03:53,165
An inscription.

51
00:03:53,165 --> 00:03:58,288
Third: the class of document on which the whole feudalism debate rests.

52
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:02,155
Land grants.

53
00:04:02,155 --> 00:04:09,625
In Mains, the examiner rewards one habit above all: naming your source and saying what kind of source it is.

54
00:04:12,033 --> 00:04:15,649
"The Arthashastra says" and "the inscription records" are not the same claim,

55
00:04:15,649 --> 00:04:18,868
and showing that you know the difference is most of the marks.

56
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:24,664
The lamps come up on the whole hall at last.

57
00:04:24,664 --> 00:04:32,028
Forty-four cases, seven rooms, and about two and a half million years, from a stone tool to a temple cut out of a hillside.

58
00:04:34,067 --> 00:04:38,438
Everything in here rests on something somebody dug up, wrote down,

59
00:04:38,438 --> 00:04:45,616
carved or buried — and where the evidence runs out, this series has tried to say so rather than fill the gap.

60
00:04:45,616 --> 00:04:47,834
Thank you for walking it with us.

61
00:04:47,834 --> 00:04:50,314
Next: Medieval India, Simply Painted.

62
00:04:50,314 --> 00:04:54,333
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

